lesson: 71
title: Some more join in: how many now
stars: 0
explain: A story can start with some things and then more things join them.
explain: To find how many there are at the end, add the ones we had to the ones that came.
explain: Tom has 8 apples and Anna gives him 5 more: 8 + 5 = 13, so Tom has 13 apples now.
vars: a=name b=name t=thing n=2..60 m=2..40
answer: n + m
exercise: {a} has {n} {t}. {b} gives {a.him} {m} more. How many {t} does {a} have now?
exercise: There are {n} {t} in a box. {a} puts {m} more {t} in the box. How many {t} are in the box now?
exercise: {a} had {n} {t}. Then {a.he} got {m} more. How many {t} does {a.he} have now?
exercise: {n} {t} were on the table. {a} brought {m} more. How many {t} are on the table now?
exercise: {a} had {n} {t}. {a.His} friend {b} gave {a.him} {m} {t}. How many {t} does {a} have now?
exercise: There were {n} {t} in a bag. {a} added {m} more. How many {t} are in the bag now?
test: After {b} hands {a} {m} {t}, how many {t} does {a} have, if {a.he} started with {n}?
test: {a} began the day with {n} {t}. In the afternoon {a.he} was given another {m}. What is {a.his} total now?
test: {m} {t} were added to a jar that already held {n}. How many {t} does the jar hold now?
test: {a} keeps {n} {t} on a shelf. Today {a.he} bought {m} more and put them there too. How many {t} are on the shelf?
exercise: {a} had {n} {t}. {b} gave {a.him} another {m}. How many {t} does {a} have altogether now?
exercise: How many {t} does {a} have now if {a.he} had {n} and then got {m} more?
exercise: In a classroom in Ohio there were {n} {t} on a shelf. The teacher put {m} more on it. How many {t} are on the shelf now?
exercise: {n} children were in a Toronto park. Then {m} more children came. How many children are in the park now?
exercise: {a} had {n.words} {t}. {b} gave {a.him} {m.words} more. How many {t} does {a} have in total now?
exercise: A bus in Dublin had {n} passengers. At the next stop {m} more got on and nobody got off. How many passengers are on the bus now?
exercise: {a} got {m} {t} for {a.his} birthday. Before that {a.he} had {n}. How many {t} does {a.he} have now?
exercise: {a} has {n} {t}. Grandma in Sydney sends {a.him} {m} more. How many {t} does {a} have now?
test: A flock of {n} sheep grazes on a hill in New Zealand. Another {m} sheep join them. How big is the flock now?
test: {a}'s collection of {n} {t} grew by {m}. What is its size now?
test: How many {t} are in a jar now, if it had {n} and {b} dropped in {m} more?
test: {m} more {t} arrived at a Glasgow school that already had {n}. How many does the school have now?
test: {b} donated {m} {t} to {a}, who already had {n}. Altogether, how many {t} does {a} have now?
test: Before lunch {a} had {n} {t}; after lunch {b} handed {a.him} {m}. What is {a.his} total now?
exercise: {a}'s got {n} {t}, {b} gives {a.him} {m} more. how many now
exercise: {n} {t} in the box, {a} chucks in {m} more. how many in there now
exercise: {a} had {n+1} — no wait, {n} {t}, then got {m} more. how many {a.he} got now
exercise: Kai says {n} plus {m} more is {n+m+1}. nah, whats the real number
test: {n} {t} on the table, {a} brings {m} more, oh and {b}'s 7 btw. how many on the table
test: {a} {n} {t}, then {m} more from {b}. how many {a.he} got
test: there was {n} {t}, {m} more come in. total now?
exam: Whereas {a} is in possession of {n} {t} and subsequently receives a further {m} from {b}, determine the quantity of {t} hereinafter held by {a}.
exam: It is given that a receptacle contains {n} {t}, to which {m} {t} are subsequently added. Ascertain the resultant contents of the receptacle.
exam: Compute the aggregate of {n.words} and {m.words}.
exam: State the total number of {t} held by {a} following the receipt of {m} {t} in addition to an original holding of {n}.
exercise: {a} has {n} dollars. {a.His} great-uncle passed away and left {a.him} {m} dollars. How many dollars does {a} have now?
exercise: When Dad was let go, the factory paid him {n} dollars of wages and a 'goodbye payment' of {m} dollars. How many dollars did he get in all?
test: {b} was 'economical with the truth', said {b} gave {a} nothing. load of rubbish — {a} had {n} {t}, {b} slipped {a.him} {m}. how many now
